window.__SLIDES&&window.__SLIDES('f1',{"s":["<section id=\"f1-tldr\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Human models · 2017–2025</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Robots adapt by modelling people, in loose threads</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px\">\n<div style=\"width:880px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Background · before the series</p><ul style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\"><li><b>Plan recognition</b> · Plan libraries and classifiers recognised human activity, but people go off-script (Hiatt et al.).</li><li><b>Cross-training, types</b> · Nikolaidis et al. (2013-15) taught robots a partner&#x27;s preference and clustered human types.</li><li><b>Bounded-memory model</b> · Nikolaidis et al. (HRI 2016) modelled how a human adapts to a robot from its last few actions.</li><li><b>Ad hoc teamwork</b> · Stone et al. asked agents to cooperate with unknown partners; type-based reasoning keeps several partner models.</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Problem</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">A robot works with a person whose goals, skill or willingness to follow are hidden and may change. It must adapt without losing trust or time.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Idea</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Keep an explicit model of the human, infer its hidden parts from what the person does, and choose or plan the robot&#x27;s behaviour from that estimate.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<img src=\"assets/img/f1_tldr.webp\" alt=\"Lineage of the key papers in this work tree, drawn by year with arrows from parent to child\" style=\"width:736px;height:260px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">Only two links reuse earlier work (solid); the other papers join by topic</p>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;background:#EFEBE0;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Terms</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#6E4B30\">Adaptability α</b> · chance a person switches to the robot&#x27;s way</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#6E4B30\">MOMDP</b> · planning model where part of the state is hidden</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#6E4B30\">Human type</b> · a person&#x27;s long-term traits, e.g. skill or stamina</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#6E4B30\">Policy library</b> · pre-built robot strategies to choose from</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Human models and mutual adaptation</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">9 papers · 2017–2025 · Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 → Lead or follow</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f1-assume\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Human models · Assumptions</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Human models assume a hidden trait the robot can infer</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#6E4B30\">A short list of human states</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Modes, 16 types, a task library or a policy library; HOTSPOT, ABPS and both Mutual adapt. papers fix the list in advance.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#6E4B30\">The trait shows in actions</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Adaptability, type, intent or lead/follow style is read from observed actions with a Bayesian update.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#6E4B30\">One human, one robot</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Each test pairs one person with one robot or agent; Lead or follow lists larger teams as future work.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#6E4B30\">The human is mostly visible</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Papers read actions or state directly; HOTSPOT relaxes this with noisy sensing and asks by speech when unsure.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#6E4B30\">The robot never errs</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Failure antic. and Lead or follow assume robot actions always succeed; no member relaxes this.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:20px;align-items:center\"><div style=\"width:760px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Builds on</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3\">Nikolaidis et al.&#x27;s 2013-16 human-adaptation models, MOMDP planning, Bayesian policy reuse and ad hoc teamwork.</p></div><svg width=\"48\" height=\"30\" viewBox=\"0 0 48 30\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,9.0 L28.799999999999997,9.0 L28.799999999999997,0 L48.0,15.0 L28.799999999999997,30.0 L28.799999999999997,21.0 L0,21.0 Z\" fill=\"#6E4B30\"/></svg><div style=\"width:816px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">So the series achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3\">Estimates of a person&#x27;s traits drive robot choices, tested from 69-person online studies to a 48-person lab study.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Human models and mutual adaptation</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">9 papers · 2017–2025 · Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 → Lead or follow</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f1-method\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Human models · Evolution</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Models grow from one trait to types, tasks and styles</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 32px\"><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.35\">Each paper picks a different hidden human variable; the shared step is inferring it online and acting on the estimate.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px\">\n<img src=\"assets/img/f1_method.webp\" alt=\"Lineage of every paper in the work tree by year, arrows from parent to child\" style=\"width:1664px;height:470px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">Each arrow points from a paper to the paper that builds on it. Dark pills mark the key papers. Papers without an arrow are tied to the series only by baseline comparisons.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Human models and mutual adaptation</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">9 papers · 2017–2025 · Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 → Lead or follow</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f1-stages\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Human models · Stages</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">The series unfolds in three stages</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:16px;align-items:stretch\"><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #C8651B;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#C8651B\">1 · Adaptation models, 2017</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2017</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7ECE3;border:2px solid #6E4B30;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Human modelling</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7ECE3;border:2px solid #6E4B30;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Shared autonomy</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7ECE3;border:2px solid #6E4B30;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Mutual adapt. &#x27;17</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">A bounded-memory human model with hidden adaptability sits inside a MOMDP; the survey maps how to model humans.</p></div><svg width=\"40\" height=\"28\" viewBox=\"0 0 40 28\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,8.4 L24.0,8.4 L24.0,0 L40.0,14.0 L24.0,28.0 L24.0,19.599999999999998 L0,19.599999999999998 Z\" fill=\"#6E4B30\"/></svg><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #3E6FD9;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#3E6FD9\">2 · Types and human state</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2019–2022</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7ECE3;border:2px solid #6E4B30;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">ABPS</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7ECE3;border:2px solid #6E4B30;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Mutual adapt. &#x27;21</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7ECE3;border:2px solid #6E4B30;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Failure antic.</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">Pick a policy from a library by human type (ABPS, &#x27;21), or turn human state into planner costs (Failure antic.).</p></div><svg width=\"40\" height=\"28\" viewBox=\"0 0 40 28\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,8.4 L24.0,8.4 L24.0,0 L40.0,14.0 L24.0,28.0 L24.0,19.599999999999998 L0,19.599999999999998 Z\" fill=\"#6E4B30\"/></svg><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #8E4FC9;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#8E4FC9\">3 · Intent, task and style</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2022–2025</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7ECE3;border:2px solid #6E4B30;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Approx. Bayes</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7ECE3;border:2px solid #6E4B30;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">HOTSPOT</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7ECE3;border:2px solid #6E4B30;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Lead or follow</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">Infer reach intent (simulation-based), a hidden task (ask when unsure) or lead/follow style (MILP allocation).</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Human models and mutual adaptation</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">9 papers · 2017–2025 · Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 → Lead or follow</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f1-s01\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Human models · Shared autonomy 2017 · 1 of 9</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Guide adaptable users, yield to stubborn ones</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">No parent in this tree. Builds on Nikolaidis&#x27; 2016 bounded-memory model, MOMDP planning and Javdani et al.&#x27;s shared autonomy.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Robot knows which of two goals is better</li><li>User switches to robot&#x27;s goal with fixed chance α</li><li>Only the robot moves the world; joystick is noisy</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Brings mutual adaptation to shared autonomy: a MOMDP infers goal and α and charges a penalty for disagreeing with the user.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/f1_s01.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Its same-group IJRR sibling applies the model to joint tasks; no later member cites it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Table clearing, 51 joystick users: 12 of 16 switched to the better goal at least once; trust above the fixed robot (p=0.010).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Human-Robot Mutual Adaptation in Shared Autonomy</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Nikolaidis et al. · HRI 2017</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f1-s02\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Human models · Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 2017 · 2 of 9</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">BAM plus MOMDP: learn adaptability, guide or comply</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Extends the authors&#x27; 2016 paper (outside tree); compares against cross-training. Sibling of Shared autonomy; neither cites the other.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Humans act in a few known modes, read from one action</li><li>Bounded memory: reacts to the last k = 3 steps</li><li>Adaptability α is fixed per person, five levels</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">BAM, a bounded-memory human model with adaptability α, is solved inside a MOMDP: one policy infers α and decides to guide or comply.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/f1_s02.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:154px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Lead or follow adopts its bounded-memory belief update; Failure antic. and Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 cite it as related work.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Table carrying, 69 online users: 57% adapted vs 26% with cross-training (p=0.036); trust above the fixed robot (p=0.048).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Human-robot mutual adaptation in collaborative tasks: Models and experiments</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Nikolaidis et al. · IJRR 2017</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f1-s03\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Human models · Human modelling 2017 · 3 of 9</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">A survey sorting human models by Marr&#x27;s three levels</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A survey, no parent in the tree. Builds on Marr&#x27;s 1982 levels, plan recognition and cognitive models such as ACT-R/E.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Robots need explicit models of human teammates</li><li>Marr&#x27;s three levels guide the choice of model</li><li>Four criteria compare models: error, timescale, structure, data</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Maps ~15 modelling approaches (the notes&#x27; own count) by level, error handling, timescale, data and structure; urges combining levels.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/f1_s03.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">ABPS cites it for imperfect human actions and the cost of modelling many behaviours; background only.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">No experiments. Finding: models that capture human error stay narrow and short-range; combining levels is proposed, untested.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Human modeling for human–robot collaboration</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Hiatt et al. · IJRR 2017</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f1-s04\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Human models · ABPS 2019 · 4 of 9</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Pick a ready-made policy by inferring the human&#x27;s type</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on Bayesian Policy Reuse (Rosman et al.) and the authors&#x27; own POMDP cobot model. Cites the Hiatt survey as background only.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>A pre-built library of 20 robot policies</li><li>16 hand-made human types, known in advance</li><li>Simulated humans; the same task repeats</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A Bayesian belief over human types, updated after each task, and expected-improvement choice of a policy from the library.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/f1_s04.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Failure antic. reuses its distracted/tired human-state idea; Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 cites it as a type-based method.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Simulated MORSE conveyor task, 300 tasks: +39.2% discounted reward and 58.8% fewer warnings than the best single policy.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Anticipatory Bayesian Policy Selection for Online Adaptation of Collaborative Robots to Unknown Human Types</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Görür et al. · AAMAS 2019</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f1-s05\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Human models · Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 2021 · 5 of 9</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Find the closest library policy, play its best partner</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on type-based ad hoc teamwork (Barrett, Stone, Albrecht; outside tree). Cites ABPS and Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 as related work only.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Two fixed, interdependent roles (bait, shooter)</li><li>The library covers the human&#x27;s way of playing</li><li>Similar policies earn similar team scores</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">No human model is learned: a cross-entropy score finds the nearest library policy; the agent plays that policy&#x27;s best partner from self-play.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/f1_s05.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">HOTSPOT cites it in related work; no later member reuses its library or cross-entropy match.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Team Space Fortress, online humans: adaptive bait beat random partner assignment by 42.3%; adaptive shooter showed no gain.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Individualized Mutual Adaptation in Human-Agent Teams</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Li et al. · IEEE THMS 2021</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f1-s06\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Human models · Approx. Bayes 2022 · 6 of 9</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Real-time reach intent by simulating hand paths</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">No parent in this tree. Builds on ABC (Rubin 1984), Zanchettin and Rocco&#x27;s analysis-by-synthesis and Hoffman&#x27;s fluency metrics.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>A known reach simulator and a motion-capture workspace</li><li>Intent is one 3D target point on the table</li><li>Hand-designed priors; turn-based pick and place</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Approximate Bayesian Computation for reach intent, made real-time by a neural surrogate and grid caching (9,100 samples in 0.01 s).</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/f1_s06.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">No later member cites it; it joins the line by topic (inferring human intent), not by inheritance.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">UR5e and a human pack 16 cubes into one box: task time 36.49 s vs 40.69 s for a turn-taking baseline.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Intuitive &amp; Efficient Human-robot Collaboration via Real-time Approximate Bayesian Inference</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Felip et al. · IROS 2022</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f1-s07\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Human models · Failure antic. 2022 · 7 of 9</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Feed the human&#x27;s state into the planner as costs</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Takes ABPS&#x27;s distracted/tired human-state idea; builds on PDDL, ROSPlan and POPF. Cites Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 as related work only.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Human capacity, knowledge and motivation are known</li><li>Only the human fails; the robot never does</li><li>Replanning always recovers from a failure</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Human state becomes action costs in a PDDL planner; a state change triggers a replan before the human&#x27;s action fails.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/f1_s07.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">No later member cites it; Lead or follow also allocates tasks, with its own cost model.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Simulated two-box task, 40 runs per case: success 25% to 100% against a human who never contributes (Case 1).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Improved Task Planning through Failure Anticipation in Human-Robot Collaboration</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Izquierdo-Badiola et al. · ICRA 2022</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f1-s08\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Human models · HOTSPOT 2024 · 8 of 9</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Infer the hidden task and ask when unsure</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on Ribeiro et al. 2021 and Melo and Sardinha&#x27;s ad hoc teamwork (outside tree). Mentions Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 in related work only.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>The robot knows a library of possible tasks</li><li>The human knows the task but may freeze or err</li><li>Partial sensing; a spoken reply may never come</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Bayesian belief over states and tasks plus a speech module; belief entropy decides between acting and asking &#x27;where are you?&#x27;.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/f1_s08.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">No later member cites it; Lead or follow also infers hidden human traits to plan, but cites other work.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Predator-Prey, 32 trials: 12.84 steps vs 10.22 for an optimal agent that knows the task (no significant gap, p&gt;0.05).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">HOTSPOT: An ad hoc teamwork platform for mixed human-robot teams</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Ribeiro et al. · PLOS ONE 2024</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f1-s09\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Human models · Lead or follow 2025 · 9 of 9</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Estimate lead/follow style and errors, then allocate</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Extends Mutual adapt. &#x27;17: reuses its bounded-memory belief update (k = 3) for two traits; also builds on the authors&#x27; RO-MAN 2022 and 2023 work.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>One human, one robot; known task graph</li><li>Preference and error-proneness change slowly</li><li>The robot is never wrong</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Two hidden traits (follow preference αf, error-proneness αe) set costs in a min-max integer program that allocates and schedules subtasks.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/f1_s09.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">48-person Fetch block task: estimates mostly track interview groups; simulation 10.93 to 9.2 wrong actions (error rate 0.8).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">To Lead or to Follow? Adaptive Robot Task Planning in Human-Robot Collaboration</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Noormohammadi-Asl et al. · T-RO 2025</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f1-eval\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Human models · Evaluation</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Eight testbeds, no shared benchmark: read each on its own</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:560px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:20px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:10px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-radius:16px;padding:22px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Benchmarks and baselines</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Nine papers, eight with experiments: joystick, online video, simulation, motion capture, real robots.</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Baselines differ: fixed robot, cross-training, best single policy, random, turn-taking, optimal agent.</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Numbers measure different things, so they cannot be ranked against each other.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;background:#F7ECE3;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Reading</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.35\">Read each row against its own baseline; the line has no common benchmark.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-radius:16px;padding:20px 32px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472;padding-bottom:4px\">Headline result of each paper</p><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#6E4B30\">Shared autonomy</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Table clearing, 51 joystick users</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">12 of 16 switched to the better goal; trust above fixed robot</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#6E4B30\">Mutual adapt. &#x27;17</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Table carrying, 69 online users</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">57% adapted vs 26% with cross-training (p=0.036)</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#6E4B30\">ABPS</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">MORSE conveyor, simulated humans</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">+39.2% reward, -58.8% warnings vs best single policy</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#6E4B30\">Mutual adapt. &#x27;21</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Team Space Fortress, online humans</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Adaptive bait beats random assignment by 42.3%</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#6E4B30\">Approx. Bayes</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">UR5e + human, 16-cube packing</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Task time 36.49 s vs 40.69 s turn-taking baseline</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#6E4B30\">Failure antic.</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Simulated box moving, 40 runs</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Success 25% to 100% against a never-helping human</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#6E4B30\">HOTSPOT</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Predator-Prey sim, Toxic Waste robot</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">12.84 steps vs 10.22 for an agent that knows the task</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#6E4B30\">Lead or follow</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">48-person Fetch block task + sim</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Estimates track styles; sim 10.93 to 9.2 wrong actions</p></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Human models and mutual adaptation</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">9 papers · 2017–2025 · Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 → Lead or follow</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f1-open\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E4B30\">Human models · Open questions</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">What the series still leaves open</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #6E4B30;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#6E4B30;width:56px\">1</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Several tests use simulated or online humans; HOTSPOT&#x27;s real trials used 7 lab members.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #6E4B30;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#6E4B30;width:56px\">2</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Traits are assumed fixed or slow; the IJRR paper says bounded memory may miss people who reflect on long histories.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #6E4B30;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#6E4B30;width:56px\">3</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Robot errors and larger human-robot teams are untested; Lead or follow lists both as limits.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Human models and mutual adaptation</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">9 papers · 2017–2025 · Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 → Lead or follow</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>"],"n":["Tree f1, &#x27;Human models and mutual adaptation&#x27;. New tree, 9 members: Shared autonomy (HRI 2017), Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 (IJRR 2017, Nikolaidis), Human modelling (IJRR 2017, Hiatt survey), ABPS (AAMAS 2019), Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 (IEEE THMS 2021, Li et al.), Approx. Bayes (IROS 2022), Failure antic. (ICRA 2022), HOTSPOT (PLOS ONE 2024), Lead or follow (T-RO 2025). The two papers titled mutual adaptation have the same short name; in tree_f1.json they are renamed with &#x27;17 and &#x27;21 so slides can tell them apart. Honest structure: the reference-list match gives only 7 member-to-member citations. Inheritance (solid in the figure, kind 1): Lead or follow -&gt; Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 (belief update and bounded memory) and Failure antic. -&gt; ABPS (human-state components). Comparison or background only (kind 2): Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 -&gt; ABPS, Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 -&gt; Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 (added by me; the base list lacked it), ABPS -&gt; Human modelling, Failure antic. -&gt; Mutual adapt. &#x27;17, HOTSPOT -&gt; Mutual adapt. &#x27;21. No kind 0 edges. Shared autonomy, Human modelling and Approx. Bayes have no solid edge; Shared autonomy is a sibling of Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 (same group, both rooted in the 2016 BAM paper, neither cites the other). Several members join by topic: modelling a human teammate&#x27;s hidden state and adapting to it (Mirsky ST2 modelling teammates, ST3/ST4 acting and adapting). Background named by members: Nikolaidis and Shah cross-training (2013), Nikolaidis et al. human types (2015) and BAM (HRI 2016), Bayesian Policy Reuse (Rosman et al.), ad hoc teamwork (Stone et al.), ABC (Rubin 1984), plan recognition and ACT-R/E (Hiatt survey). Survey words: Albrecht and Stone method families, type-based reasoning (Mutual adapt. both, ABPS, HOTSPOT, Lead or follow) and goal recognition (Approx. Bayes). Uncertain: see the uncertain list.","Shared assumptions, with the paper that relaxes each. 1. A short, known list of human states: modes with five adaptability levels (Shared autonomy, Mutual adapt. &#x27;17), 16 hand-made types and 20 policies (ABPS), 24 library policies (Mutual adapt. &#x27;21: 13 bait and 11 shooter), a task library (HOTSPOT), one 3D reach target (Approx. Bayes), 11 values of two traits (Lead or follow). 2. Hidden traits show in actions and are inferred with Bayes-style updates: BAM belief (Shared autonomy, Mutual adapt. &#x27;17, Lead or follow), type belief (ABPS, HOTSPOT), ABC (Approx. Bayes). Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 avoids a human model and uses cross-entropy matching. 3. One human, one robot or agent: stated as a limit by Lead or follow; all tests are dyads. 4. The human is mostly visible: Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 treats modes as fully observable; Shared autonomy allows noisy joystick input; HOTSPOT relaxes this with noisy sensing and speech; ABPS uses six boolean signals from simulated activity recognition. 5. The robot is never wrong: Failure antic. and Lead or follow state it; none relaxes it. Other assumptions: traits are fixed or change slowly (Shared autonomy, Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 fix alpha per person; ABPS tests a type switch every 30 tasks, in simulation); Failure antic. assumes the human state is known (simulated) and replanning always recovers. Source: each member&#x27;s assumption slide and notes (p72, p73, p74, p75, p59, p77, p78, p88, p89). The notes mark some assumption lists as the notes author&#x27;s reading (Approx. Bayes and Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 have no explicit assumptions section).","Three stages, grouped by what the paper adds; they are a reading order, not a citation chain. Stage 1 (2017): Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 introduces BAM inside a MOMDP (hidden adaptability alpha, bounded memory k = 3, SARSOP offline). Shared autonomy applies the same idea with k = 1 and a disagreement penalty when only the robot moves the world. Human modelling is a survey that sorts human models by Marr level; no algorithm. Stage 2 (2019-2022): ABPS keeps 20 pre-solved policies and updates a belief over 16 types, choosing by expected improvement. Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 skips the human model: cross-entropy finds the nearest library policy and its best partner is played. Failure antic. turns capacity, knowledge and motivation into PDDL action costs and replans on change. Edge: Failure antic. -&gt; ABPS (kind 1, human-state components). Stage 3 (2022-2025): Approx. Bayes infers reach intent by simulation (ABC with a neural surrogate, 9,100 samples in 0.01 s). HOTSPOT infers a hidden task, planning between task and information-gathering Q-functions by belief entropy. Lead or follow returns to the BAM belief update (kind 1 edge to Mutual adapt. &#x27;17) to estimate follow preference and error-proneness, then solves two MILPs. Pattern: the hidden variable changes (adaptability, type, state, intent, task, style) while the loop stays similar: observe, update a belief, act. This is the notes&#x27; reading; only two inheritance edges back it up. Tags from the notes: type-based reasoning (Mutual adapt. both, ABPS, HOTSPOT, Lead or follow), planning (Shared autonomy, Mutual adapt. &#x27;17, ABPS, Failure antic., HOTSPOT, Lead or follow), goal inference (Approx. Bayes).","Three stages, grouped by what the paper adds; they are a reading order, not a citation chain. Stage 1 (2017): Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 introduces BAM inside a MOMDP (hidden adaptability alpha, bounded memory k = 3, SARSOP offline). Shared autonomy applies the same idea with k = 1 and a disagreement penalty when only the robot moves the world. Human modelling is a survey that sorts human models by Marr level; no algorithm. Stage 2 (2019-2022): ABPS keeps 20 pre-solved policies and updates a belief over 16 types, choosing by expected improvement. Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 skips the human model: cross-entropy finds the nearest library policy and its best partner is played. Failure antic. turns capacity, knowledge and motivation into PDDL action costs and replans on change. Edge: Failure antic. -&gt; ABPS (kind 1, human-state components). Stage 3 (2022-2025): Approx. Bayes infers reach intent by simulation (ABC with a neural surrogate, 9,100 samples in 0.01 s). HOTSPOT infers a hidden task, planning between task and information-gathering Q-functions by belief entropy. Lead or follow returns to the BAM belief update (kind 1 edge to Mutual adapt. &#x27;17) to estimate follow preference and error-proneness, then solves two MILPs. Pattern: the hidden variable changes (adaptability, type, state, intent, task, style) while the loop stays similar: observe, update a belief, act. This is the notes&#x27; reading; only two inheritance edges back it up. Tags from the notes: type-based reasoning (Mutual adapt. both, ABPS, HOTSPOT, Lead or follow), planning (Shared autonomy, Mutual adapt. &#x27;17, ABPS, Failure antic., HOTSPOT, Lead or follow), goal inference (Approx. Bayes).","Paper: Nikolaidis, Zhu, Hsu, Srinivasa, HRI 2017 (notes p72). Edges: no parent in this tree and no child. Its model comes from the 2016 BAM conference paper (outside the tree), Ong et al. MOMDPs and Javdani et al. The IJRR paper of the same year (Mutual adapt. &#x27;17) is a sibling: it extends the 2016 paper and does not cite this one (checked in its reference list). Setting: only the robot moves the world; joystick input does not change state. A penalty C = -0.32 is charged for disagreement (R_opt = 11, R_sub = 10). Memory k = 1, alpha in {0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1}, 520 MOMDP states, solved with SARSOP. Numbers (Sec. 7.1-7.2): n = 51, between-subjects, 3 trials. Mutual adaptation 16 people: 12 of 16 (75%) guided the robot to the optimal goal in at least one trial, 9 of 16 (56%) in the final trial. One-way (follows the user) 17 people: only 2 of 17 changed goals. Team score: mutual beats one-way (p = 0.001); the fixed robot scores highest by design. Trust M = 3.94 (SE 0.18), collaboration M = 3.91; trust above the fixed-goal robot p = 0.010. Caveats: one task with two goals; generalisation is argued, not tested. The no-adaptation group size is not stated (51-16-17 = 18 by subtraction, left out). &#x27;Changed goals&#x27; for the one-way group means switching during the task. Survey vocabulary: Mirsky ST2/ST4 with an adaptive-teammate variation; Albrecht-Stone type-based reasoning plus POMDP planning.","Paper: Nikolaidis, Hsu, Srinivasa, IJRR 2017 (notes p73). Edges: parent is the 2016 conference paper (outside tree); no parent inside. Children: Lead or follow (kind 1: adopts the bounded-memory adaptation model and its belief update); Failure antic. (kind 2: related work, it only picks among predefined policies); Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 (kind 2: cited in passing as one relaxation of normative human models). Not linked to Shared autonomy. Method: the human follows a mode and switches to the robot&#x27;s mode with probability alpha; MOMDP observable part = task step plus recent modes, hidden part = alpha (5 values). Table task 734 x 5 = 3670 states, hallway 340 x 5 = 1700, solved with SARSOP offline, online execution. Numbers (Sec. 7.2, Fig. 10a): study 1 n = 69, three conditions; 57% vs 26% adapted (chi-square(1, N = 46) = 4.39, p = 0.036); the bar counts (about 13/10 and 6/17) are read from the chart. Trust above the Fixed robot U = 180, p = 0.048; non-inferior to cross-training on trust and collaboration. Repeated trials (n = 43): only 13% of non-adapters switched later. Transfer (n = 58): adaptation in the first task predicts the second (p = 0.021, Nagelkerke R2 = 0.119, weak). Hallway-only study n = 65: 48% adapted. Caveats: online video study with a button interface, short interactions, Mechanical Turk workers; compared to cross-training only on adaptation and ratings, not fluency. k = 1 pilot (30 people) misread 23% of samples. Limit stated: BAM may not suit people who reflect on long histories.","Paper: Hiatt, Narber, Bekele, Khemlani, Trafton, IJRR 2017 (notes p74). A survey with no experiments, baselines or numbers. Edges: parent none in tree; child ABPS (kind 2: cited for the point that intention-aware models grow costly with more behaviours, and that human actions may be imperfect). Content: Marr&#x27;s levels (computational = what people do, algorithmic = how they think incl. errors and timing, implementational = physical realisation) and four criteria (noisy processing, timescale, hand-coded vs learned, training data). Approaches covered include simple probabilistic models, CNNs, k-NN, HMMs and Markov planning, dynamic Bayesian networks, grammar models, ACT-R/E, mReasoner, MAC/FAC, Leabra and Spaun. The count of ~15 is the notes author&#x27;s count from Figs 1-2, not a number in the paper. Findings (Sec. 6.1): level correlates with how noisy human processing is handled; models robust to noisy observations work best over long timescales; algorithmic models need little data but are hand-written, narrow and short-range (&#x27;the biggest downside&#x27;). Recommendation (Sec. 6.2): combine levels, e.g. an HMM whose states are ACT-R/E models; not tested. Caveat: the selection is called somewhat subjective and not a comprehensive review. Survey vocabulary: related to Mirsky ST2, predates both surveys&#x27; framing. It is the only member that does not propose a method, and it joins the line by topic.","Paper: Gorur, Rosman, Albayrak, AAMAS 2019 (notes p75). Edges: parent Human modelling (kind 2, background). Children: Failure antic. (kind 1: takes the capacity, distracted and tired human-state components and merges them into a motivation cost); Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 (kind 2: described in related work as defining human types a priori, e.g. expertise and stamina). Its own method parents (Bayesian Policy Reuse, the authors&#x27; POMDP model) are outside the tree. Method: offline, 20 policies are solved from randomly varied POMDP models and 16 known human types are simulated (20 policies x 16 types x 50 tasks = 16,000 interactions); online, after every task the belief over types is updated from 6 boolean signals and the next policy is chosen by expected improvement. Numbers (Table 1, Experiment 2: 10 unseen types, type switches every 30 tasks, 300 tasks per scenario): reward 4.62 vs 3.32 (+39.2%), warnings 31 vs 75.2 (-58.8%), success 0.92 vs 0.84 (+9.5%), task time 26.04 s vs 30.49 s (-14.6%); all p &lt; 0.0001 by ANOVA. Experiment 1: with one unknown human ABPS matches the best policy&#x27;s regret after about 6 tasks (p = 0.895 for the later difference). Caveats: the only baseline is the best single policy in hindsight; everything is simulated, real users are future work; the &#x27;Total warnings&#x27; label in Table 1 may be a per-scenario mean (read as given). Survey vocabulary: Mirsky ST2/ST4; Albrecht-Stone type-based reasoning (policy reuse).","Paper: Li, Ni, Agrawal, Jia, Raja, Gui, Hughes, Lewis, Sycara, IEEE THMS 2021 (notes p59; venue taken from the file name, not printed in the PDF). Edges: parents ABPS and Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 (both kind 2: related-work mentions; ABPS as a priori human types, the IJRR paper as one relaxation of normative models). Child HOTSPOT (kind 2). Its method parents (Barrett and Stone policy reuse, Albrecht and Ramamoorthy type-based reasoning, Nikolaidis et al. 2015 clustering) are outside the tree. The paper uses the term mutual adaptation for human and agent adapting to each other, but the mechanism differs from BAM. Method: library of 13 bait and 11 shooter policies (RL, rule-based mirror, SQIL imitation); self-play table of mean fortress kills per minute; the human&#x27;s recent moves are scored against each library policy by cross-entropy, the closest policy C is found, its best partner D is played. Numbers (Sec. VI): Exp. 1, 104 online players: correlation of closeness-to-best-partner with team score r = 0.636 (baits) and r = 0.834 (shooters). Exp. 2, 134 players, 15 trials: human shooter with agent bait, Adaptive vs Random t(25.8) = 2.87, p = .008; vs Fixed p = .122; trials 6-8 higher than both (p &lt; .01). Human bait with agent shooter: no significant difference. Caveats: the paper does not say which team type the 42.3% is measured in or the baseline score (the Discussion ties it to the adaptive bait); small groups (about 10-20 after cleaning); the text once says 12 policies but Table I shows 13 + 11; roles must be clearly separated. Survey vocabulary: Mirsky ST2/ST4, adaptive teammates; Albrecht-Stone type-based reasoning.","Paper: Felip, Gonzalez-Aguirre, Nachman (Intel Labs), IROS 2022 (notes p77; arXiv version, venue from the file name). Edges: none in either direction. The paper does not cite any other member (checked reference list); it shares the topic of inferring a human&#x27;s hidden intent from observed motion, which is Mirsky ST2 / Albrecht-Stone goal recognition, and is the only member that predicts physical reaching. Method: ABC-reject: sample a target from a prior (near the human, along the gaze, at detected objects), simulate a 3 s reach with a 9-DoF arm model, keep targets whose simulated path is close to the observed path. A 3-layer surrogate trained on 10,000 simulated paths (mean error 1.7 cm) evaluates 9,100 samples in 0.01 s instead of 1365 s (&gt;100,000x); the grid is cached. Numbers (Table I, 10 runs per method, 16 pick-and-place each): task time solo human 57.05 s, solo robot 64.55 s, turn-taking baseline 40.69 s, intent prediction 36.49 s; functional delay -0.85 s to -2.91 s; robot idle 1.09 to 0.62 s; human idle 2.02 to 0.97 s (units read as seconds from Fig. 6). Caveats: number of human participants not stated; no significance test; the +- values are not defined; how &#x27;enough certainty&#x27; to start a reach is thresholded is not given; the surrogate may not generalise to other kinematics. The assumptions list is the notes author&#x27;s reading.","Paper: Izquierdo-Badiola, Canal, Rizzo, Alenya, ICRA 2022 (notes p78). Edges: parents ABPS (kind 1: Sec. III says the intuition builds on the capacity and the distracted and tired components of Gorur et al., joined into one motivation cost, plus Devin and Alami&#x27;s knowledge component) and Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 (kind 2: related work, &#x27;only deal with decision making on previously defined policies&#x27;). No children. Method: three costs per agent, each 0 to 10 (capacity depends on location; knowledge and motivation are general); the cost of an action is their sum; the plan controller watches states, waits for the robot&#x27;s current action, cancels, updates costs and replans through ROSPlan and POPF. Human success probability is 1 - cost / max cost. Numbers (Table I, mean of 40 simulations per case): Case 1 (human never contributes) success 25.00% to 100.00%, failed actions 44.5% to 8.5%, replans 3.75 to 0.93; Case 2 (random contribution) success 37.5% to 95.0%, failed actions 36.8% to 11.0%, replans 3.13 to 1.48. Actions given to the human drop (65.6% to 31.9% in Case 1). Caveats: simulation only, one two-box scenario, human states and failures simulated not sensed; no other planner is compared and no variances or statistical tests are reported; the cost formula is shown only for the move action. Survey vocabulary: Mirsky ST3; planning given a human-state model (tag confidence medium).","Paper: Ribeiro, Henriques, Colcher, Duarte, Melo, Milidiu, Sardinha, PLOS ONE 2024 (notes p88). Edges: parent Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 (kind 2: related work, an adaptive agent using a cross-entropy match to a pre-trained policy library; nothing reused). Method parents (Ribeiro et al. 2021, Melo and Sardinha, PLASTIC) are outside the tree. HOTSPOT joins the line mostly by topic: a robot adapting to an unknown human, with partial observability and communication added. Method: Bayesian state estimator and task inference over a library of M tasks; planning mixes a task Q-function and an information-gathering Q-function weighted by (1 - entropy) and entropy; speech-to-text, entity recognition and node identification form the sensing side, spoken questions the acting side. Numbers: Predator-Prey (exact, 32 trials, 626 states): optimal full view 6.75, optimal task known 10.22, HOTSPOT 12.84, random 76.06 steps; 10.22 vs 12.84 not significant. Toxic Waste (real robot, 9 real trials with 7 lab members plus 32 simulated, 260 states): about 12 steps real, 13 simulated, 10 optimal, 63 random; about 39 vs 13 steps without the locate-human query (significant, p = 0.000656). These Toxic Waste values are approximate readings of bars. Live speech accuracy 58.62% recognition, 77.77% entity, 74.07% node. Caveats: small human sample, turn-based play, participants told the rules, tabular methods do not scale; the name TEBOPA is never expanded. Survey vocabulary: partial observability and communication variations; Mirsky ST2+ST3.","Paper: Noormohammadi-Asl, Smith, Dautenhahn, T-RO 2025 (notes p89; arXiv v2, venue from the file name). Edges: parent Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 (kind 1: Sec. IV-B adopts the bounded-memory adaptation model and the belief update over observable and hidden state to estimate human adaptability). No other member is cited; its related work (Nemlekar, Fiore, Gombolay, Liu, Cheng, Pupa) is outside the tree. It is the only member that clearly inherits from another. Method: belief over alpha_f (0 leads, 1 follows) and alpha_e (error-proneness) on 11 values, updated from the last k = 3 actions; allocation MILP minimises the larger of the two agents&#x27; costs; a second MILP schedules; solved with Gurobi. Directing a human who likes to lead costs extra; leaving an error-prone human unguided costs extra. Numbers: 48 analysed participants (58 recruited), 144 tasks. Pattern B, the hardest: higher following estimate (H(2) = 6.5, p = 0.039) and more subtasks assigned by the robot (p = 0.042). Simulation (15 runs per combination, P_follow = 0.3): wrong human actions at true error rate 0.8: non-adaptive 10.93 vs adaptive 9.2; subtasks given to the human at error rate 0.1: 8.93 vs 6.8. No significant difference at P_follow = 0.9. Caveats: no single headline percentage; the interview-group counts in Fig. 11 (15/19/4/3 + 3 + 1 = 45) differ from the text (17/20/4/3) and from 48; no real-participant comparison against a non-adaptive robot; participants mostly young students (24.0 +- 3.9 years); the cost parameters are not given. Survey vocabulary: Mirsky ST2 (estimating human style) with ST3/ST4.","Numbers as written in each paper&#x27;s own notes; none are comparable. Shared autonomy (HRI 2017): n = 51 joystick users; 12 of 16 switched to the better goal in at least one trial, 9 of 16 in the last; one-way robot 2 of 17; trust above fixed robot p = 0.010. Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 (IJRR 2017): n = 69 online; 57% vs 26% adapted (p = 0.036); trust above Fixed p = 0.048; bar counts read from the chart. Human modelling: survey, no numbers. ABPS (AAMAS 2019), simulation, 300 tasks per scenario: reward 4.62 vs 3.32 (+39.2%), warnings 31 vs 75.2 (-58.8%), success 0.92 vs 0.84, task time 26.04 s vs 30.49 s. Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 (THMS 2021): Exp. 2, 134 players; adaptive vs random t = 2.87, p = .008; headline 42.3% (baseline not defined precisely). Approx. Bayes (IROS 2022): task time 36.49 s vs 40.69 s baseline, 57.05 s solo human, 64.55 s solo robot; participant count not stated. Failure antic. (ICRA 2022): success 25.00% to 100.00% (Case 1) and 37.50% to 95.00% (Case 2), 40 simulations per case. HOTSPOT (PLOS ONE 2024): Predator-Prey 6.75 / 10.22 / 12.84 / 76.06 steps (optimal full view, optimal task known, HOTSPOT, random). Toxic Waste values (~12, ~13, ~10, ~63; ~39 vs ~13 without the query) are approximate bar readings. Lead or follow (T-RO 2025): 48 participants; Pattern B following estimate p = .039, subtasks assigned p = .042; simulation 10.93 vs 9.2 and 8.93 vs 6.8. The interview-group counts disagree between figure and text. Reading: baselines range from a fixed robot to cross-training, a best single policy, random play and turn-taking, so a larger number in one row says nothing about another. Open limits: simulated or online humans, small real samples, robots assumed never wrong, dyads only.","Numbers as written in each paper&#x27;s own notes; none are comparable. Shared autonomy (HRI 2017): n = 51 joystick users; 12 of 16 switched to the better goal in at least one trial, 9 of 16 in the last; one-way robot 2 of 17; trust above fixed robot p = 0.010. Mutual adapt. &#x27;17 (IJRR 2017): n = 69 online; 57% vs 26% adapted (p = 0.036); trust above Fixed p = 0.048; bar counts read from the chart. Human modelling: survey, no numbers. ABPS (AAMAS 2019), simulation, 300 tasks per scenario: reward 4.62 vs 3.32 (+39.2%), warnings 31 vs 75.2 (-58.8%), success 0.92 vs 0.84, task time 26.04 s vs 30.49 s. Mutual adapt. &#x27;21 (THMS 2021): Exp. 2, 134 players; adaptive vs random t = 2.87, p = .008; headline 42.3% (baseline not defined precisely). Approx. Bayes (IROS 2022): task time 36.49 s vs 40.69 s baseline, 57.05 s solo human, 64.55 s solo robot; participant count not stated. Failure antic. (ICRA 2022): success 25.00% to 100.00% (Case 1) and 37.50% to 95.00% (Case 2), 40 simulations per case. HOTSPOT (PLOS ONE 2024): Predator-Prey 6.75 / 10.22 / 12.84 / 76.06 steps (optimal full view, optimal task known, HOTSPOT, random). Toxic Waste values (~12, ~13, ~10, ~63; ~39 vs ~13 without the query) are approximate bar readings. Lead or follow (T-RO 2025): 48 participants; Pattern B following estimate p = .039, subtasks assigned p = .042; simulation 10.93 vs 9.2 and 8.93 vs 6.8. The interview-group counts disagree between figure and text. Reading: baselines range from a fixed robot to cross-training, a best single policy, random play and turn-taking, so a larger number in one row says nothing about another. Open limits: simulated or online humans, small real samples, robots assumed never wrong, dyads only."]});
